🔹 What PSA Does
PSA controls how pods are allowed to run based on predefined security rules.
It ensures:

✅ Pods follow security best practices
✅ No unsafe configurations (like root access, privileged containers)
✅ Standardized security across namespaces

🔹 Three PSA Security Levels
PSA provides three predefined profiles:
1. 🔓 Privileged (Least Secure)
No restrictions
Allows everything (root, privileged mode, host access)

👉 Used for:
System-level workloads (like CNI plugins)


2. 🔐 Baseline (Moderate Security)
Prevents known risky configurations
Allows standard workloads

👉 Restricts:
Privileged containers
Some host-level access


3. 🔒 Restricted (Most Secure)
Enforces strict security best practices

👉 Requires:
Run as non-root
No privilege escalation
Read-only filesystem (recommended)


🔹 How PSA Works
PSA is applied using namespace labels.
✅ Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: secure-ns
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted


🔁 Modes of Enforcement
PSA supports 3 modes:

Mode           Description
enforce        Blocks pods that violate policya
audit          Allows but logs violation
sarn          Allows but shows warnings

“In PSA, audit mode logs policy violations without blocking pods, while warn mode allows pods but shows warnings to users, 
helping teams gradually adopt stricter security policies.”

labels:
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: baseline
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: baseline


